@node Keys
@chapter Keys

@table @kbd
@item C-`
@kindex C-`
Summon, focus, or hide the current context's terminal.

@item C-u C-`
@kindex C-u C-`
Create another quake terminal in the current context.

@item C-c t n
@kindex C-c t n
Create another quake terminal in the current context.

@item C-c t N
@kindex C-c t N
Create another fullscreen terminal in the current context.

@item C-c t b
@kindex C-c t b
Choose a terminal in the current context.

@item C-c t ]
@kindex C-c t ]
Select the next terminal, wrapping within the current context.

@item C-c t [
@kindex C-c t [
Select the previous terminal, wrapping within the current context.

@item C-c t f
@kindex C-c t f
Toggle between a quake panel and the full editing area.

@item C-c t g
@kindex C-c t g
Toggle a global terminal, even inside a project.

@item C-c t G
@kindex C-c t G
Create another global quake terminal.

@item C-c t t
@kindex C-c t t
Choose from all managed project and global terminals.
@end table

@node Display Modes
@chapter Display Modes and Editor Layouts
@cindex quake panel
@cindex fullscreen terminal
@cindex display mode preferences
@cindex editor layout restoration

The top panel occupies approximately 35% of the frame height.  Customize
@code{jg-ghostel-quake-height} to change that fraction.  Full display fills
the Emacs editing area; it does not change OS fullscreen state.  Shrinking
or hiding restores the editor layout.

Each terminal remembers its display mode for its lifetime: after expanding
with @kbd{C-c t f}, the toggle alternates between hidden and full-frame.
Press @kbd{C-c t f} again to return to quake mode; subsequent hide/show
toggles then retain quake mode.  Reopening fullscreen captures the current
editor layout, so editor changes made while hidden are preserved.

Each terminal owns its display mode; the frame owns the editor layout.
Switching, cycling, hiding, and either completion picker leave mode
preferences unchanged and display the destination in its own remembered
mode.  Switching fullscreen to quake restores the editor and opens the
destination in the top panel; quake to fullscreen saves the editor layout
and expands the destination.  Switching between terminals with the same
mode simply replaces the buffer.

New terminals always start in quake mode with @kbd{C-c t n} or a prefix
toggle, even when created from a fullscreen terminal.  Use @kbd{C-c t N}
to create a fullscreen terminal instead.  Only explicit creation and
@kbd{C-c t f} choose a terminal's mode; displaying it never inherits or
changes another terminal's preference.

@cindex multiple frames
Window layouts are independent per frame; terminal buffers and their
remembered preferences are shared across frames.  Changing the preference
in one frame does not resize an already visible view in another frame;
that view uses the preference when next summoned or selected through the
terminal commands.  Hiding a view never overwrites the preference.
Preferences last for the terminal session, not across Emacs restarts.

@node Contexts
@chapter Project and Global Contexts
@cindex project terminals
@cindex global terminals
@cindex creation-time ownership
@cindex shell directory changes
@cindex TRAMP

Inside a managed terminal, its creation-time ownership determines the
context.  Otherwise, the current @code{project.el} project determines it;
outside projects, the global group is used.  Project terminals start at
the project root; global terminals start in @file{~}.  Shell @command{cd}
and buffer renaming do not change ownership.  Projects are distinguished
by their full roots, including TRAMP prefixes, not just names.

@cindex completion picker
The completion picker annotates each terminal with its owner and Ghostel's
process status/title, using the normal Vertico/Orderless setup.  @kbd{C-c t b}
is scoped to the current context; @kbd{C-c t t} includes all managed contexts.
@xref{Keys}.

@cindex most recently used terminal
@cindex terminal lifetime
The toggle reuses the most recently used terminal in that group.  An
unfocused, already visible matching panel is focused rather than hidden.
Hiding leaves processes running; terminal exit or buffer killing closes
the view and restores the editor.  While a panel is open, editor splits
can still be changed normally.

The Ghostel action in the project-switch menu uses this same workflow and
always shows the selected project's terminal.  Existing
@code{ghostel-project} terminals are adopted by identity.  Plain
@kbd{M-x ghostel} terminals and Ghostel compilation/exec buffers remain
outside the managed groups and are still accessible via @kbd{C-x b}.

@node Input Modes
@chapter Input Modes and Reserved Keys
@cindex input modes
@cindex reserved keys
@cindex char mode

The terminal controls work in semi-char, char, copy, Emacs, and line input
modes.  Even in char mode, @kbd{C-`} and the @kbd{C-c} / @kbd{C-u} prefixes
are reserved for Emacs.  Use @kbd{C-c C-c} for interrupt and @kbd{C-c C-q}
to send a reserved key literally.  On TTY connections that cannot encode
Ctrl-backtick distinctly, the named command @kbd{M-x jg-ghostel-toggle}
remains available outside char mode.

GPTel remains on @kbd{C-c SPC SPC}; its old @kbd{C-`} binding is removed.

@node Tests
@chapter Tests
@cindex tests
@cindex headless tests
@cindex native shell tests

Run these commands from @file{private_dot_config/private_emacs/} in the
dotfiles source checkout, where @file{Taskfile.yaml} is located:

@example
task test-ghostel
task test-ghostel-gui
task test-info
@end example

@code{test-ghostel} loads installed Ghostel Lisp without its native module,
substitutes only shell creation, and exercises real windows, identities,
and keymaps.  Multi-frame tests are skipped in headless batch mode.

@code{test-ghostel-gui} runs the same suite plus real-shell integration
tests in an isolated Xvfb display, including multiple frames and native
line-mode entry.  It requires @command{xvfb-run}, @command{xauth}, and a
working installed Ghostel native module.  Neither task loads or changes
the running Emacs configuration.

@code{test-info} exercises the real chezmoi installer in a temporary source
and destination, including unchanged applies, source changes, deleted output,
failed builds, and preservation of unrelated local manuals.  It verifies
native Emacs discovery, indexes, and registration after the directory has
already been cached.  It requires @command{chezmoi}, @command{python3}, GNU
Texinfo, and installed Emacs packages.  Run it from the source checkout:
it needs the repository's installer template, not only deployed files.

Override @env{EMACS} for a non-snap installation, and @env{EMACS_DIR} to
select a package directory (packages are read from
@file{$EMACS_DIR/var/elpa}).  The snap default uses its underlying binary
because the wrapper requires a display even in batch mode.

@node Building the Manual
@chapter Building and Registering the Manual
@cindex building the manual
@cindex Info directory

This manual's maintained source is @file{docs/ghostel.texi}.  On
@command{chezmoi apply}, @file{run_after_build-emacs-info.sh.tmpl} runs after
the source is deployed and generates @file{var/info/jg-ghostel.info} and
its local @file{var/info/dir} catalog.  This state directory follows the
configuration's @code{no-littering} layout.  Compilation happens during
chezmoi deployment, not during Emacs startup.

The installer requires GNU Texinfo's @command{makeinfo} and
@command{install-info}, plus the standard @command{sha256sum} and
@command{cmp} utilities.  The package lists for new chezmoi configurations
include @code{texinfo} on Arch/Ubuntu and @code{install-info} on Ubuntu.
Older configurations must already provide these commands or refresh their
package data before deployment; a missing command causes a clear error.

A source checksum in @file{var/info/jg-ghostel.sha256} detects content
changes even when file timestamps are preserved.  Unchanged applies leave
the generated manual and catalog untouched.  Missing output is rebuilt,
including when the whole generated directory has been deleted.  Builds
are staged before replacement, so a compiler failure leaves the last good
manual usable.  Catalog updates preserve unrelated local manual entries.
Generated state is excluded from both Git and chezmoi source management.

The source declares an @code{Emacs} directory category and a
@code{Ghostel Workflow} menu entry.  @command{install-info} registers that
entry in the local @file{dir} file; no system directory or root access is
needed.

@file{lisp/init/jg-core.el} registers the state directory lazily through
@code{Info-additional-directory-list}, preserving Emacs's system and
package manuals.  It also invalidates the buffer-local directory caches
when reloaded, so existing Info buffers see a newly registered directory.

After applying the configuration, use @kbd{C-h i}, then @kbd{d} if Info
reopened a previous node, and select @code{Ghostel Workflow} from the
@code{Emacs} section.  The menu command is @kbd{m Ghostel Workflow RET}.
Within this manual, @kbd{i} searches its key and concept indexes.
Preview the generated file directly with @kbd{C-u C-h i}, supplying the
path to @file{var/info/jg-ghostel.info}.

For the underlying mechanisms, see the GNU Texinfo manual's
@uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/manual/texinfo/html_node/Installing-Dir-Entries.html,
Installing Info Directory Files} and
@uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/manual/texinfo/html_node/Other-Info-Directories.html,
Info Files in Other Directories}, and chezmoi's
@uref{https://www.chezmoi.io/user-guide/use-scripts-to-perform-actions/,
script documentation}.

